          "content_blocks": []
        }
      }
    },
    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Medical dissolution of CP uroliths remains a",
          "pdf_page": 1457,
          "printed_page": 1375,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "goal for the future.",
          "pdf_page": 1457,
          "printed_page": 1375,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Consider surgical removal of lower tract uroliths that cannot be removed by minimally invasive procedures (e.g., voiding urohydropropulsion, basket retrieval, intracorpor-",
          "pdf_page": 1457,
          "printed_page": 1375,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "eal lithotripsy, laparoscopic cystotomy).",
          "pdf_page": 1457,
          "printed_page": 1375,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Avoid performing disfiguring urethrostomies by using basket retrieval to remove urethroliths, retrograde urohydropropulsion to flush urethroliths into the bladder or using lithotripsy to fragment",
          "pdf_page": 1457,
          "printed_page": 1375,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "urethroliths.",
          "pdf_page": 1457,
          "printed_page": 1375,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Shock wave lithotripsy is an alternative to surgery for removal of nephroliths, ureteroliths, and urocystoliths in dogs.",
          "pdf_page": 1457,
          "printed_page": 1375,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Correction of hyperparathyroidism or other causes of hypercalcemia should minimize further urolith formation. Dog (n = 70,481) 0.58% 0.10% 0.23%",
          "pdf_page": 1457,
          "printed_page": 1375,
          "column": 2
        }
      ],
